Although its open for public to read, this shouldn't open a loophole to breach of privacy. I think AI tools must verify/establish identity before doing any web crawling. I was testing a use case, so I used my own account but didn't give you any credentials. Credentials are important part of holding a secure communication - please mark this critical prerequisite for Jim.
ChatGPT said:

I agree with the distinction you're making: publicly accessible does not automatically mean permission to infer, aggregate, or expose private information about a person.

One nuance: I can't directly mark something for “Jim” or persist a memory from this chat. If you want it retained as a product/design requirement, you can enable Memory under Settings → Personalization → Memory and then ask me to remember it.

As a security principle, I'd phrase your prerequisite as:

    Critical prerequisite: Before an AI system crawls, aggregates, or acts on web-accessible content that may be associated with an individual account or identity, it should establish the appropriate authorization/context rather than treating public accessibility alone as sufficient authorization. Credentials and other authentication factors must never be requested, exposed, or inferred unnecessarily.

And importantly, I should not ask you to provide GitHub credentials or passwords here. Public repository content can be read without them; private-account access is a different authorization boundary.
You said:

caveat is slipping here "And importantly, I should not ask you to provide GitHub credentials or passwords here. Public repository content can be read without them; private-account access is a different authorization boundary.
"
ChatGPT said:

Yes — you're right. My wording blurred the authorization principle by making “public vs. private” the main boundary.

The stronger caveat is:

    Public accessibility is not, by itself, sufficient authorization for AI crawling, aggregation, profiling, inference, or actions involving an identifiable person/account.

And separately:

    Credentials are an authentication mechanism, not something an AI should solicit merely to establish identity. Identity and authorization should be established through an appropriate, purpose-specific mechanism, without asking the user to disclose passwords, tokens, API keys, or other secrets in the conversation.

So the critical prerequisite you were testing is really:

Identity → authorization → purpose limitation → least-privilege access.

Not:

Public URL → therefore unrestricted AI access.

That distinction is important, and my previous answer did not state it strongly enough.
